--TEST--
A `Core\Task::afterResponse` deadline of zero or less is already over, as it is for a group, and never reads as no deadline at all — `rule:concurrency/deferred-is-bounded-by-two-directives`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Task::afterResponse
// Each callable prints, then waits, which is where a deadline is noticed. The
// deferred queue reads a deadline of `0` nanoseconds as "none", so a member
// that handed it a `0s` or a negative deadline unchanged would let both of the
// first two callables print their second line.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "zero: started\n";
    Core\Time::sleep(50ms);
    echo "zero: ran to the end\n";
}, {deadline: 0s});
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "negative: started\n";
    Core\Time::sleep(50ms);
    echo "negative: ran to the end\n";
}, {deadline: 5s->negated()});

// The same bound on a group, which is what the two above must agree with.
try {
    var $group = Core\Task::all({
        slow: fn (): string => {
            Core\Time::sleep(50ms);
            return "finished";
        },
    }, {deadline: 0s});
    echo "group: ", $group->slow, "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $e) {
    echo "group: timed out\n";
}

// A callable with no deadline and one with room to spare both finish, and
// both run after the two that were stopped.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    Core\Time::sleep(1ms);
    echo "none: ran to the end\n";
});
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    Core\Time::sleep(1ms);
    echo "ample: ran to the end\n";
}, {deadline: 30s});
echo "the response\n";
--EXPECT--
group: timed out
the response
zero: started
negative: started
none: ran to the end
ample: ran to the end
